  <HOMEWORK title="DDR Homework 6: The controller thinks every read just times out"
            prompt="CK, the command bus, and chip select are all clearly toggling - a scope on the bus shows completely normal-looking activity - but the built-in memory test reports almost nothing but timeouts: it waits its full timeout budget on nearly every read, then gives up and counts it as a miss. Writes look like they're happening. What would make a chip see activity but still miss its own selection?"
            hint="CS/CA training aligns *when* a chip samples chip-select against the rest of the command bus - it isn't automatic. Check this chip's own CS timing-error setting: is its chip-select sampling actually aligned with the real signal, or still lagging from before training ran?"
            explanation="CS/CA training was simply never run on this chip: its own chip-select sampling lags the real signal by most of a unit interval. A read command is only asserted for a single unit interval before the controller moves on - with CS sampling arriving this late, the chip's own &quot;am I selected&quot; check doesn't catch up until that window has already closed, so it misses the read command outright, the controller polls for a reply that will never come, and eventually times out and records a mismatch. Note that writes do go through here: the write burst holds CS asserted continuously across many consecutive unit intervals, so a timing lag that would miss a single brief pulse doesn't matter once CS has been high for a while - which is exactly why &quot;writes look like they're happening&quot; was a real clue, not a red herring. Real DDR5/LPDDR5 CA/CS training exists precisely to find and correct this kind of per-chip sampling skew before relying on short, individually-addressed commands like reads."/>
</LABCHAIN>
